'''Jade Baliscans''' ({{Lang-bl|Baliscanos de Jade}}), also known as '''''tusán''''' (a [[W:Loanword|loanword]] from [[Daras|Darasian]]; {{zh|tr=[[wikt:土生|土生]]|p=tǔ shēng|j=tou2 saang1|l=local born}}), are Baliscan citizens whose ancestors came from the [[Jade peoples|Jade countries]]. Jade Baliscans constitute a subgroup of [[East Fosian Baliscans]] which also constitute a subgroup of [[Fosian Baliscans]]. Many Jade Baliscans have ancestors from [[Zong]], [[Daras]], [[Jumia]], [[Sunbon]], [[Limonia]] and [[Fenia]], as well as other regions that are inhabited by large populations of the [[Jade diaspora]], especially [[Northeast Fosia]] and some other countries such as [[Okaiken]], [[Kwangju]], [[Nusaraya]], and the [[Haitan|Haitan subcontinent]]. 

Due to [[W:Acculturation|acculturation]], most third and fourth generation Jade Baliscans do not speak the language of their ancestors. However, many second generation Jade Baliscans can speak one or more [[W:Varieties of Chinese|varieties of Jade]] that may include [[W:Mandarin Chinese|Darasian]], [[W:Yue Chinese|Sunbonese]], [[W:Hakka Chinese|Fenian]] and [[W:Southern Min|Northern Zong]] ([[W:Hokkien|Zong]]), in addition to [[Baliscano]] or other local languages.

In the [[Census in Balisca|2020 census in Balisca]], 4.8 million people claimed ''tusán'' or Jade ancestry. However, according to a 2024 study by the [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS), it was estimated that 5-10% (or 7-15 million) Baliscans had Jade roots and ancestry, tracing back to the 19th century arrival of the first 100,000 Jumian immigrants that migrated to [[Bayara]] and entered relationships with many local women.

